const mainHtml = "<section class=\"chapter\">\r\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\r\n        <div class=\"chapter-head\">\r\n          <h1>Preservation Before Compilation</h1>\r\n          <p class=\"ch-sub\">2.2 The Compilation under the Khalifas &middot; how the Qur&rsquo;an was safeguarded before any single book existed</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <p class=\"lede prose-col\">The Qur&rsquo;an was complete &mdash; every verse revealed &mdash; the moment the Prophet died in 11 AH. It was not, however, a book. It lived in the memories of hundreds of companions and on scattered fragments of bone, bark and parchment. Two Caliphs, a generation apart, turned that living memory into the single fixed text every printed Qur&rsquo;an in the world today still matches, letter for letter.</p>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"box box-predict\">\r\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Predict before you read</p>\r\n          <p>Two separate compilation efforts happened within about fifteen years of each other &mdash; one under Abu Bakr, one under &rsquo;Uthman. If the Qur&rsquo;an had already been written down once under Abu Bakr, why would &rsquo;Uthman need to do the job again? Write a guess. The answer is not &ldquo;the first copy was lost or wrong&rdquo; &mdash; hold that thought.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Preserved twice over, from the very first day</h3>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">During the Prophet's own lifetime the Qur&rsquo;an was already being preserved through two parallel, deliberate methods, not left to chance.</p>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\">The first was memorisation. Companions known as the <strong>huffaz</strong> (those who had memorised the entire text) numbered in the hundreds by the time of the Prophet's death, and the Prophet himself reviewed the whole revealed Qur&rsquo;an with Jibreel once every Ramadan &mdash; and, significantly, <em>twice</em> in the final Ramadan of his life, which companions later understood as a sign his death was near.</p>\r\n        <p class=\"prose-col\">The second was writing. The Prophet appointed dedicated <strong>Scribes of the Revelation</strong> (Kuttab al-Wahy) &mdash; among them a young Ansari companion named <strong>Zayd ibn Thabit</strong>, who will matter enormously in the next two lessons &mdash; to record each verse as it was revealed, on whatever material was to hand: palm-leaf stalks, thin flat stones, shoulder blades of animals, pieces of leather and parchment. What did <em>not</em> yet exist was a single bound volume gathering every verse in one place, in a fixed order, between two covers. There was a simple reason for that: while the Prophet was alive, revelation was still arriving, and verses could still be abrogated or reordered by divine instruction (naskh) &mdash; binding it into one finished book before revelation itself was finished would have made no sense.</p>\r\n\r\n        <div style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 0;\" class=\"prose-col\">\r\n          <h3 style=\"display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap;\">Ready to practise?</h3>\r\n          <p style=\"margin-top:10px; color:var(--ink-soft);\">A short Practice Quiz first, then a graded Quiz once you're confident.</p>\r\n        </div>\r\n\r\n        <div class=\"lesson-pager\">\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link\" href=\"../../2-1/index.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">&larr; Back</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Unit 2.1</div></a>\r\n          <a class=\"pager-link next\" href=\"practice.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">Next &rarr;</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Practice Quiz</div></a>\r\n        </div>\r\n      </div>\r\n    </section>";
